{"context":"The original feature list asked for screen sharing, a collaborative terminal, and remote command execution, motivated by two legitimate use cases from the vision: remote troubleshooting and collaborative debugging. Remote command execution is the highest-risk item in the entire feature set — it would place an execution endpoint on every device on the tailnet, including embedded devices, reachable by anyone the ACL admits. It also duplicates a capability the team already has: SSH over Tailscale, with ACL-based authorization, audit and session recording already built and reviewed.","decision":"**walkie will not implement remote command execution.** Remote shell access is delegated to Tailscale SSH.\n\nFor the collaborative-debugging use case, walkie will offer **read-only PTY streaming**: a viewer receives the sharer's terminal output, with no input path back to the sharer's shell. If bidirectional collaboration is genuinely needed later, walkie will **wrap tmux** rather than reimplement a terminal multiplexer.\n\nThis is a decision to decline a requested feature, recorded so that the decline is discoverable and citable rather than appearing as an omission.","consequences":"**Positive.** The remote-troubleshooting use case is served by a mature, audited tool instead of a new one. walkie never becomes an execution surface, which means an authentication or authorization defect in walkie cannot escalate to arbitrary code execution across the fleet. And the security review surface stays small enough for a small team to actually cover.\n\n**Negative, and it is a real cost.** Troubleshooting now involves two tools rather than one, so the quickstart documentation must point users to Tailscale SSH explicitly — otherwise the capability will simply look missing. The 'collaborative terminal' and 'remote command execution' items from the original feature list are declined and partially redirected, and that must be communicated to the team rather than left to be discovered.\n\n**Read-only PTY streaming remains in scope** as a later phase, and carries its own smaller risk that will need its own treatment: terminal output can contain secrets, so a sharer must be able to see that they are sharing and stop it instantly.","alternativesConsidered":"**Build it with hard constraints** — an allowlist of named commands with no shell interpolation, opt-in per device and off by default, interactive confirmation on the target for every invocation, execution as an unprivileged user, timeouts and output-size caps, and a full audit log. This is roughly the strongest form the feature could take, and it was still rejected: it reimplements SSH with a newer, less-reviewed, less-audited implementation. A second remote-execution path is a net security regression, not an added feature — the safest version of a redundant execution endpoint is still worse than not having one.\n\n**Read-only observation only, and nothing else.** Retained, and scheduled as a later phase rather than declined.\n\n**Declining screen sharing entirely as well.** Rejected as over-correction: read-only output streaming carries a fraction of the risk of an input path and directly serves collaborative debugging, which is a stated use case."}